{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BmhU87viQ9FcMJycbuFG3E","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tTemporal abstraction via variable-rate imagination promises to extend the effective planning horizon of world-model reinforcement learning, and $\\Delta t$-conditioning has proven effective in model-predictive control (MPC) methods. We find that this approach cannot be directly transferred to imagination-based actor-critic training: every variable-rate configuration of DreamerV3 we test degrades performance. Through factorial diagnostics on two continuous-control tasks (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run), we identify the combination of trained temporal embedding and multi-rate data exposure as the causal factor and characterize the resulting failure mode: \\emph{conditioning-induced value overestimation} (CIVO). The trained embedding enables the reward head of the recurrent state-space model (RSSM) to develop a $2.78\\times$ prediction gap between $\\Delta t{=}1$ and $\\Delta t{=}2$, which the critic internalizes as optimistic values, universally degrading performance to below 30\\% of baseline across all six tested seeds in two environments ($n{=}6$). Gradient decomposition reveals that the observation decoder channel, not the reward head, carries the critical $\\Delta t$-specific spatial information that drives the fidelity gap. Removing multi-rate data exposure suppresses CIVO entirely (5 seeds, all below the $1.5\\times$ diagnostic threshold); full-pathway gradient stopping reduces CIVO severity in one of two Walker seeds (peak NVE $4.12\\times$ to $1.66\\times$) but eliminates it in neither (0/3 across environments), exposing mechanisms beyond gradient-mediated co-adaptation. A lightweight online detector identifies all five CIVO cases (100\\% sensitivity) with advance warning before performance degradation in two cases, enabling early intervention without a reference baseline. The contrast with MPC methods identifies the feedback architecture (imagination-based bootstrapping versus re-planning) as the structural factor that transforms manageable model imprecision into pathological value dynamics. Targeted gradient stopping on the identified obs-decoder pathway fails to prevent CIVO (1/6 seeds across two environments), confirming that CIVO operates through redundant gradient channels that no single-pathway intervention addresses. A three-level intervention hierarchy (gradient, output, representation) reveals that no tested intervention reliably eliminates CIVO. Representation-level noise (TCN) is the only tested intervention that achieves complete CIVO suppression in any configuration (2--3 of 4 Walker Walk seeds at $\\sigma{=}0.2$), but exhibits high seed sensitivity and fails to transfer across environments, positioning it as a diagnostic probe that identifies the representation level as the relevant intervention target rather than a deployable mitigation.\n2\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EAzBaRRX7k7Uw8sQVoaivG","session_id":"8160c2d3-e1f0-4475-97c0-9800d500973c","uuid":"b1ad5a3f-7818-4756-803a-e493fb245891","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:48:19.331Z"}
