{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EAzBaRRX7k7Uw8sQVoaivG","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Core assessment:** The paper rigorously diagnoses a specific failure mode (CIVO) in DreamerV3 with flat dt-conditioning, using a well-designed factorial methodology. However, the novelty is incremental relative to existing MBRL overestimation literature, and the contribution is narrow in scope.\n\n**Main strengths:**\n- The MPC-vs-imagination architectural boundary is the paper's most distinctive and actionable insight\n- Clean factorial design with well-chosen ablation controls (frozen embedding, Gumbel gate)\n- CIVO is credibly differentiated from standard maximization bias and delusional bias\n- The gradient-magnitude vs causal-importance finding is a useful empirical data point\n\n**Main weaknesses (Major):**\n1. Core novelty is a specific instance of well-known MBRL model exploitation, not a new failure class\n2. Standard countermeasures (Double Q, ensemble critics) are argued against but never experimentally tested -- the strongest evidence for CIVO's distinctiveness is missing\n3. Director (Hafner et al., 2022) -- the canonical hierarchical Dreamer extension -- is entirely absent from the paper despite being directly relevant\n\n**Key missing references:** Director, Plan2Explore. TD-M(PC)^2 is in the bib but uncited in text.\n\n**Recommendation:** The paper needs either (a) a working mitigation demonstrating deep mechanistic understanding, or (b) substantially broader experimental coverage beyond two DMC tasks on one architecture to justify a top-venue publication."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a2fdf50da562db8d3 (use SendMessage with to: 'a2fdf50da562db8d3' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 91536\ntool_uses: 22\nduration_ms: 183211</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8160c2d3-e1f0-4475-97c0-9800d500973c","uuid":"be3d8738-0f34-4469-9859-a36445e271e2","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:51:01.375Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Paper summary\n\n**Title**: \"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in DreamerV3: A Diagnostic Analysis of Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate Imagination\"\n\nThe paper investigates why adding temporal step size Δt conditioning to DreamerV3's RSSM world model fails. It identifies \"conditioning-induced value overestimation\" (CIVO) as the primary failure mechanism: the trained Δt-embedding enables the critic to exploit imprecise multi-step RSSM predictions, creating a positive feedback loop between value overestimation and actor exploitation.\n\n## Key claims\n\n1. Trained Δt-embedding is the necessary condition for CIVO (0/17 causal control seeds collapse, 10/13 trigger seeds exhibit CIVO)\n2. NVE > 1.5× is a reliable diagnostic (AUROC=0.969, n=29, leave-one-env-out CV AUROC 0.92-0.97)\n3. Seven interventions across three levels fail: gradient stopping (1/17), output anchoring (0/2), representation noise (2/8)\n4. MPC vs imagination architectural dependency — TAWM succeeds with Δt-conditioning under MPC but DreamerV3 fails under imagination-based AC training\n\n## Related work as presented in the paper\n\nThe paper covers four areas in Section 2:\n\n**Temporal abstraction in RL**: Options framework [Sutton et al., 1999], learned options [Bacon et al., 2017], macro-actions [Durugkar and Stone, 2016], FiGAR [Sharma et al., 2017], action repeats [Lakshminarayanan et al., 2017]. These operate in model-free settings and modify action space rather than dynamics model.\n\n**World-model RL**: Dreamer family [Hafner et al., 2019, 2020, 2023], DreamerV3's symlog + categorical value. Other architectures: IRIS [Micheli et al., 2022], TransDreamer [Chen et al., 2022], TWM [Robine et al., 2023].\n\n**Variable-rate planning in world models**: TAWM [Nhu et al., 2025] conditions TD-MPC2's latent dynamics on temporal step size under MPC. Their success contrasts with the paper's negative findings under imagination-based AC training.\n\n**Value overestimation in MBRL**: MBPO [Janner et al., 2019] — model error → value overestimation, short-horizon rollouts as mitigation. Delusional bias [Lu et al., 2018]. Double Q-learning [van Hasselt et al., 2016], ensemble critics [Chen et al., 2021]. Paper argues CIVO differs because it arises from interaction between learned temporal conditioning signal and imprecise multi-step latent predictions.\n\n## Discussion section highlights\n\n- **Section 5.1 (MPC vs Imagination)**: TAWM comparison presented as \"empirical observation consistent with architectural dependency\" rather than causal claim. Table 19 compares TAWM vs DreamerV3 structure. Acknowledges confounds (obs space, planning horizon, critic structure, Δt range).\n- **Section 5.2 (Gradient magnitude vs causal importance)**: Reward head has largest gradient fraction (38-49%) but blocking it is a no-op. Obs-decoder has smallest fraction (16-22%) but is causally critical. Broader lesson: gradient magnitude ≠ causal importance.\n- **Section 5.3 (Standard countermeasures)**: Discusses why Double Q, Clipped Double Q, ensemble critics, MBPO truncation weren't implemented. Double Q requires redesigning λ-return pipeline. Ensemble critics most promising but requires restructuring categorical distribution aggregation. MBPO truncation irrelevant (already using H=8-15).\n- **Section 5.4 (Limitations)**: Environment diversity (all DMC proprioceptive), statistical power (2-4 seeds), single architecture (RSSM only), NVE in-sample calibration.\n\n## Paper structure\n\n13 pages total. Abstract, Introduction (contributions 1-4), Background/Related Work, Method (base architecture, variable-rate imagination, factorial design, diagnostic metrics), Results (11 subsections covering cross-environment CIVO, ablation matrix, NVE diagnostic, causal control, multiple pathways, RSSM fidelity, intervention hierarchy, baselines, validation), Discussion (4 subsections), Conclusion, References (16 citations).\n\n## Your task\n\nWrite a detailed review focusing on **Novelty and Related Work**. Evaluate:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is the CIVO finding? Is diagnosing a failure mode in a specific architecture a sufficient contribution? Is the NVE diagnostic metric novel? Is the intervention hierarchy framework novel? Is the dual-pathway observation (A: CIVO vs B: IRD) novel?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are important related works missing? Is the positioning relative to TAWM fair and complete? Are there other relevant works on value overestimation, temporal abstraction in world models, or negative results in RL?\n3. **Contribution significance**: Would the community benefit from this work? Does it provide actionable insights? Is \"structured negative evidence\" a sufficient contribution for a top venue?\n4. **Positioning and framing**: Is the paper framed appropriately as a diagnostic/negative-result paper? Are claims well-scoped? Does the title match the content?\n5. **Broader impact**: Does this work open new research directions or primarily close off dead ends?\n\nConsider these specific novelty questions:\n- Value overestimation in MBRL is well-studied (MBPO, delusional bias). What specifically is new about CIVO beyond \"it happens in this specific setting\"?\n- The gradient magnitude ≠ causal importance finding (Section 5.2) — is this well-known or genuinely novel?\n- The dual collapse pathways (A: value-driven CIVO, B: eval-driven IRD) — is this characterized rigorously enough to be a contribution, or is it descriptive?\n- The paper doesn't try ensemble critics or Double Q. Section 5.3 discusses engineering barriers, but a reviewer might ask: shouldn't at least a simplified version be tried?\n\nMissing related works to check for:\n- THICK (hierarchical world models)\n- Hieros (hierarchical imagination)\n- Director (goal-conditioned hierarchical planning in DreamerV3)\n- Plan2Explore and related exploration methods\n- TD-MPC and TD-MPC2 (beyond just TAWM)\n- Other works on multi-step prediction error in latent models\n- Model-based overestimation beyond MBPO (e.g., STEVE, conservative model-based methods)\n\nStructure your review as:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (numbered list with brief explanations)\n- **Weaknesses** (numbered list, labeled Major/Minor, with specific suggestions)\n- **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n- **Novelty Assessment** (itemized by contribution)\n- **Scores**: Soundness (1-4), Contribution (1-4), Clarity (1-4), Overall (1-6), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is a diagnostic/negative-result paper — judge it on whether it provides genuinely new insights rather than just confirming expected behavior.","agentId":"a2fdf50da562db8d3","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is a summary of the key findings:\n\n**Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\n**Core assessment:** The paper rigorously diagnoses a specific failure mode (CIVO) in DreamerV3 with flat dt-conditioning, using a well-designed factorial methodology. However, the novelty is incremental relative to existing MBRL overestimation literature, and the contribution is narrow in scope.\n\n**Main strengths:**\n- The MPC-vs-imagination architectural boundary is the paper's most distinctive and actionable insight\n- Clean factorial design with well-chosen ablation controls (frozen embedding, Gumbel gate)\n- CIVO is credibly differentiated from standard maximization bias and delusional bias\n- The gradient-magnitude vs causal-importance finding is a useful empirical data point\n\n**Main weaknesses (Major):**\n1. Core novelty is a specific instance of well-known MBRL model exploitation, not a new failure class\n2. Standard countermeasures (Double Q, ensemble critics) are argued against but never experimentally tested -- the strongest evidence for CIVO's distinctiveness is missing\n3. Director (Hafner et al., 2022) -- the canonical hierarchical Dreamer extension -- is entirely absent from the paper despite being directly relevant\n\n**Key missing references:** Director, Plan2Explore. TD-M(PC)^2 is in the bib but uncited in text.\n\n**Recommendation:** The paper needs either (a) a working mitigation demonstrating deep mechanistic understanding, or (b) substantially broader experimental coverage beyond two DMC tasks on one architecture to justify a top-venue publication."}],"totalDurationMs":183211,"totalTokens":91536,"totalToolUseCount":22,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4027,"cache_read_input_tokens":87134,"output_tokens":374,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4027},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":374,"cache_read_input_tokens":87134,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4027,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4027,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":11,"searchCount":5,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":6}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Qgs6GWacirTGAKQhNrG8YY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have both independent reviews. Let me proceed with the roundtable process:\n\nStep 1 (Independent reviews) - DONE\nStep 2 (Cross-sharing) - I need to share each reviewer's findings with the other\nStep 3 (Convergence) - Identify agreements and disagreements\nStep 4 (Synthesis) - Final combined assessment\n\nLet me get the full R2 review by sending a message to continue that agent, then proceed with Step 2 cross-sharing.\n\nActually, looking at the R2 output, it seems like the agent gave a summary rather than the full review. \n\nI need to request the complete review from R2 before moving forward.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9231,"cache_read_input_tokens":59332,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9231},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8160c2d3-e1f0-4475-97c0-9800d500973c","uuid":"1b384bbf-c481-4a5d-8db7-55f48e99168f"}
