{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019iZdbEJc5BoQULXRCcCwBQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Lfn84GiAJe7thLVUQMdHvL","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/docs/paper/sections/abstract_body.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11242,"cache_read_input_tokens":43112,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":11242,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01C87x6XK22ZFfR1vd3R88hn","session_id":"8160c2d3-e1f0-4475-97c0-9800d500973c","uuid":"8fcd279c-0cf6-453b-8751-d7ee95430213"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Lfn84GiAJe7thLVUQMdHvL","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tTemporal abstraction via variable-rate imagination promises to extend the effective planning horizon of world-model reinforcement learning, and $\\Delta t$-conditioning has proven effective in model-predictive control (MPC) methods. We find that this approach cannot be directly transferred to imagination-based actor-critic training: every variable-rate configuration of DreamerV3 we test degrades performance. Through factorial diagnostics on two continuous-control tasks (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run), we identify the combination of trained temporal embedding and multi-rate data exposure as the causal factor and characterize the resulting failure mode: \\emph{conditioning-induced value overestimation} (CIVO). The trained embedding enables the reward head of the recurrent state-space model (RSSM) to develop a $2.78\\times$ prediction gap between $\\Delta t{=}1$ and $\\Delta t{=}2$, which the critic internalizes as optimistic values, universally degrading performance to below 30\\% of baseline across all six tested seeds in two environments ($n{=}6$). Gradient decomposition reveals that the observation decoder channel, not the reward head, carries the critical $\\Delta t$-specific spatial information that drives the fidelity gap. Removing multi-rate data exposure suppresses CIVO entirely (5 seeds, all below the $1.5\\times$ diagnostic threshold); full-pathway gradient stopping reduces CIVO severity in one of two Walker seeds (peak NVE $4.12\\times$ to $1.66\\times$) but eliminates it in neither (0/3 across environments), exposing mechanisms beyond gradient-mediated co-adaptation. A lightweight online detector identifies all five CIVO cases (100\\% sensitivity) with advance warning before performance degradation in two cases, enabling early intervention without a reference baseline. The contrast with MPC methods identifies the feedback architecture (imagination-based bootstrapping versus re-planning) as the structural factor that transforms manageable model imprecision into pathological value dynamics. Targeted gradient stopping on the identified obs-decoder pathway fails to prevent CIVO (1/6 seeds across two environments), confirming that CIVO operates through redundant gradient channels that no single-pathway intervention addresses. A three-level intervention hierarchy (gradient, output, representation) reveals that no tested intervention reliably eliminates CIVO. Representation-level noise (TCN) is the only tested intervention that achieves complete CIVO suppression in any configuration (2--3 of 4 Walker Walk seeds at $\\sigma{=}0.2$), but exhibits high seed sensitivity and fails to transfer across environments, positioning it as a diagnostic probe that identifies the representation level as the relevant intervention target rather than a deployable mitigation.\n2\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01C87x6XK22ZFfR1vd3R88hn","session_id":"8160c2d3-e1f0-4475-97c0-9800d500973c","uuid":"e79d4fd5-3ff1-454c-9960-36d76a63383c","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:51:42.295Z"}
