{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DNTGCdW5mioRH5Vmf8DikF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Ea6qBLVFmnJ5AVzBopRktk","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_9/projects/event-gated-adaptive-horizon/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T210241/best/paper.tex","limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2160,"cache_read_input_tokens":90265,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2160,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01C87x6XK22ZFfR1vd3R88hn","session_id":"8160c2d3-e1f0-4475-97c0-9800d500973c","uuid":"f18d358a-6abb-4518-9f20-f5d8a65fd8f9"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ea6qBLVFmnJ5AVzBopRktk","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[10pt,letterpaper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsfonts}\n4\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n5\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n8\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n9\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n10\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n11\t\\usepackage{array}\n12\t\\usepackage{caption}\n13\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n14\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n15\t\n16\t\\newcolumntype{C}{>{\\centering\\arraybackslash}X}\n17\t\\newcolumntype{L}{>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{Diagnostic Analysis of Value Overestimation from Temporal Abstraction in DreamerV3}\n20\t\n21\t\\author{\n22\tAnonymous Authors\n23\t}\n24\t\n25\t\\date{}\n26\t\n27\t\\begin{document}\n28\t\\maketitle\n29\t\n30\t%% ============================================================\n31\t%%  ABSTRACT\n32\t%% ============================================================\n33\t\\begin{abstract}\n34\tTemporal abstraction---acting at variable time scales---is a long-sought capability in reinforcement learning, yet integrating it into world-model agents remains poorly understood.\n35\tWe present a systematic diagnostic analysis of variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3, identifying \\emph{Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation} (CIVO) as a fundamental failure mode.\n36\tThrough controlled factorial experiments across four environments (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, Hopper Hop), we establish a three-link causal chain: (1)~a \\emph{trained} temporal embedding learns dt-specific representations on RSSM states whose fidelity degrades at $\\mathrm{dt}>1$ (reward prediction gap $2.78\\times$); (2)~the critic bootstraps from these imprecise-but-confidently-conditioned imagined states, producing pathological value overestimation (Normalized Value Excess $\\geq 1.5\\times$ in 9/9 trained-embedding seeds, 0/12 untrained; AUROC $= 0.969$); (3)~the actor exploits overestimated value regions, triggering an irreversible positive-feedback collapse.\n37\tA $2\\times 2$ ablation matrix confirms that \\emph{both} embedding training and exposure to $\\mathrm{dt}>1$ data are necessary conditions for CIVO.\n38\tWe further evaluate a systematic \\emph{Intervention Hierarchy} spanning gradient-level (Selective Gradient Stopping, 1/17 seeds), output-level (Reality-Anchored Value Estimation, 0/2), and representation-level (Temporal Conditioning Noise, 2/8) interventions across 42 seeds, 8 methods, and 3 environments.\n39\tNo post-hoc intervention reliably eliminates CIVO, establishing that architectural solutions---such as hierarchical temporal abstraction---are necessary for safe variable-rate imagination in world-model RL.\n40\tWe additionally identify that gradient magnitude is decoupled from causal importance in multi-head world models (reward head: 38--49\\% of gradients but causally irrelevant; observation decoder: 16--22\\% but causally critical), a methodological insight with implications beyond this work.\n41\t\\end{abstract}\n42\t\n43\t%% ============================================================\n44\t%%  1  INTRODUCTION\n45\t%% ============================================================\n46\t\\section{Introduction}\n47\t\n48\tTemporal abstraction---the ability to reason and act at multiple time scales---is a foundational goal in reinforcement learning~\\citep{sutton1999options,precup2000temporal}.\n49\tWorld-model agents such as DreamerV3~\\citep{hafner2023dreamerv3} offer a natural testbed: one can modify the temporal granularity of \\emph{imagined} rollouts without changing environment interaction, enabling controlled study of variable-rate planning.\n50\tRecent work on Temporal-Aware World Models (TAWM;~\\citealt{nhu2025tawm}) demonstrates that dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC-based planning (TD-MPC2), motivating the question of whether similar conditioning can benefit imagination-based actor-critic architectures.\n51\t\n52\tAll of our attempts at variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3---whether with fixed $\\mathrm{dt}=2$, $\\mathrm{dt}=4$, or a learned gate---degrade performance relative to the $\\mathrm{dt}=1$ baseline.\n53\tThis paper provides a systematic factorial diagnosis explaining \\emph{why}.\n54\t\n55\tWe identify \\textbf{Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation (CIVO)}: a pathological positive-feedback loop in which a trained temporal embedding produces confident but inaccurate conditioning on RSSM states whose prediction quality degrades at $\\mathrm{dt}>1$.\n56\tThe critic bootstraps from these states, systematically overestimating value; the actor exploits overestimated regions, further biasing training data; the cycle accelerates until performance collapses.\n57\tWe introduce the \\textbf{Normalized Value Excess (NVE)} metric---the ratio of experiment value-to-eval over baseline value-to-eval at the same training step---and show that $\\text{NVE} \\geq 1.5\\times$ is a reliable early-warning diagnostic (AUROC $= 0.969$, Youden-optimal threshold independently converging to $1.53\\times$).\n58\t\n59\tOur contributions are threefold:\n60\t\\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n61\t\\item \\textbf{CIVO causal chain}: We establish, via a $2\\times 2$ ablation matrix (trained/frozen $\\times$ exposed/unexposed), that both embedding training and exposure to $\\mathrm{dt}>1$ data are jointly necessary for CIVO. The NVE$\\geq 1.5\\times$ marker achieves 9/9 detection rate for trained embeddings and 0/12 false positives for untrained configurations (AUROC $= 0.969$).\n62\t\\item \\textbf{MPC vs.\\ imagination architectural boundary}: dt-conditioning succeeds in MPC (TAWM) but fails in imagination-based actor-critic. We localize the vulnerability to critic bootstrapping---MPC re-plans from real states, breaking the positive-feedback loop that imagination sustains.\n63\t\\item \\textbf{Intervention Hierarchy}: A systematic evaluation of 8 methods across 3 intervention levels (gradient, output, representation) and 3 environments with 42 seeds. No post-hoc method reliably eliminates CIVO. Gradient magnitude is decoupled from causal importance---reward gradients dominate (38--49\\%) but reward-only intervention fails worst; observation-decoder gradients are smallest (16--22\\%) but causally critical.\n64\t\\end{enumerate}\n65\t\n66\t%% ============================================================\n67\t%%  2  BACKGROUND AND RELATED WORK\n68\t%% ============================================================\n69\t\\section{Background and Related Work}\n70\t\n71\t\\paragraph{Temporal abstraction in RL.}\n72\tThe options framework~\\citep{sutton1999options,precup2000temporal} formalizes temporally extended actions.\n73\tFiGAR~\\citep{sharma2017figar} learns action repetition counts.\n74\tAction repeat is standard practice in continuous control~\\citep{hafner2019planet,hafner2020dream}.\n75\tThese approaches modify the \\emph{policy} temporal granularity; we instead modify the \\emph{imagination} granularity within a world model.\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{World-model RL.}\n78\tDreamerV3~\\citep{hafner2023dreamerv3} learns a Recurrent State-Space Model (RSSM) and trains actor-critic purely in imagination.\n79\tRelated architectures include PlaNet~\\citep{hafner2019planet}, Dreamer~\\citep{hafner2020dream}, DreamerV2~\\citep{hafner2021dreamerv2}, IRIS~\\citep{micheli2023iris}, and TWM~\\citep{robine2023twm}.\n80\tTAWM~\\citep{nhu2025tawm} extends TD-MPC2 with dt-conditioning for variable-rate MPC planning and reports success; our work identifies why the same conditioning mechanism fails in imagination-based actor-critic.\n81\t\n82\t\\paragraph{Value overestimation in MBRL.}\n83\tModel-Based Policy Optimization (MBPO;~\\citealt{janner2019mbpo}) identifies model error as a source of value overestimation and proposes short-rollout truncation.\n84\t\\citet{lu2018delusional} formalize ``delusional bias'' from policy-class constraints.\n85\tOur CIVO mechanism is distinct: it arises from model imprecision amplified by learned temporal conditioning, not from policy-class restrictions or uniform model error.\n86\tStandard countermeasures (Double Q-learning~\\citep{hasselt2010doubleq,fujimoto2018td3}, ensemble critics~\\citep{chen2021redq}) are non-trivial to apply in DreamerV3's 255-bin categorical critic architecture.\n87\t\n88\t\\paragraph{Reproducibility in deep RL.}\n89\t\\citet{henderson2018matters} document the outsized role of random seeds in deep RL.\n90\t\\citet{agarwal2021deep} advocate interquartile mean (IQM) and stratified bootstrap confidence intervals.\n91\tWe report all seeds individually and use NVE as a seed-level diagnostic.\n92\t\n93\t%% ============================================================\n94\t%%  3  METHODOLOGY\n95\t%% ============================================================\n96\t\\section{Methodology}\n97\t\n98\t\\subsection{DreamerV3 with Variable-Rate Imagination}\n99\t\n100\tWe extend DreamerV3's imagination rollouts to support variable temporal strides $\\mathrm{dt} \\in \\{1, 2, 4\\}$."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01C87x6XK22ZFfR1vd3R88hn","session_id":"8160c2d3-e1f0-4475-97c0-9800d500973c","uuid":"d0cc0755-12d4-4561-b24c-dde2d8453206","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:52:09.094Z"}
