{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W7ZvyxSaaBtbgomtVQCDw6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260520T210241\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: Weak Reject (3/6)\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-20T21:50:46.220242+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t\n9\t# Review: R2 — Novelty and Related Work\n10\t\n11\t## Summary\n12\t\n13\tThis paper diagnoses why adding temporal step-size (dt) conditioning to DreamerV3's RSSM world model fails under imagination-based actor-critic training, identifying \"conditioning-induced value overestimation\" (CIVO) as the failure mechanism. Through factorial experiments on two DMC tasks, it isolates trained dt-embedding + multi-rate data exposure as jointly necessary for CIVO, proposes an NVE diagnostic metric, tests a three-level intervention hierarchy (all insufficient), and contrasts the failure with TAWM's success under MPC. The paper is framed as structured negative evidence with diagnostic methodology.\n14\t\n15\t## Strengths\n16\t\n17\t1. **Well-differentiated positioning relative to TAWM.** The MPC-vs-imagination distinction is cleanly articulated: dt-conditioning works in TD-MPC2 because MPC re-plans from real states, whereas imagination-based training creates a closed feedback loop. This is a genuinely useful insight for practitioners considering variable-rate world models. The paper correctly identifies this as an empirical observation rather than a causal proof, appropriately scoping the claim.\n18\t\n19\t2. **Comprehensive literature map behind the scenes; compact but adequate related work in text.** The related work section (though only two paragraphs) covers the key bases: temporal abstraction (options through TAWM), value overestimation (Thrun through MOPO/MOReL/COMBO), compounding error, and diagnostic methodology precedents (Henderson 2018). The citations to THICK, Hieros, and LS-Imagine as hierarchical alternatives are well-placed and correctly differentiate flat dt-conditioning from hierarchical approaches. The inclusion of DreamerV4 is forward-looking.\n20\t\n21\t3. **The factorial design is methodologically sound and follows established precedent.** Citing Henderson (2018) and implicitly following Andrychowicz et al. (2021) in spirit, the 2x2 causal matrix (trained/removed x exposed/unexposed) is clean. The frozen-embedding control (015b) and Gumbel-gate control (022b) are particularly well-designed ablations that isolate trainability as the necessary condition.\n22\t\n23\t4. **The gradient-magnitude != causal-importance finding is underappreciated.** While the general principle that gradient magnitude can be misleading is known in multi-task learning (PCGrad, CAGrad), the specific demonstration in the Dreamer world-model context — where the reward head dominates gradients (38-49%) but is causally irrelevant, while the observation decoder (16-22%) carries the critical signal — is a concrete, well-quantified instance that adds empirical substance to the general principle.\n24\t\n25\t5. **CIVO is mechanistically distinct from prior overestimation work.** The paper makes a credible case that CIVO differs from standard maximization bias (addressed by Double Q / TD3), from delusional bias (policy-class-induced), and from MBPO-style model exploitation (mitigated by rollout truncation). The key distinction — that the RSSM is \"confidently wrong\" at dt>1, producing low-variance biased predictions that ensemble disagreement would not flag — is well-argued in the Discussion.\n26\t\n27\t## Weaknesses\n28\t\n29\t### Major\n30\t\n31\t1. **The core novelty is narrower than the framing suggests.** The paper presents five contributions, but several are incremental rather than independently novel:\n32\t   - CIVO itself is a specific instance of a known phenomenon: model exploitation in MBRL (MBPO, Lambert et al. 2020, Frauenknecht et al. 2025). The novelty lies in identifying the *specific trigger* (trained dt-embedding co-adaptation) rather than the *phenomenon* (value overestimation from model error). The paper should more explicitly acknowledge that it is characterizing a specific instance of a well-understood failure class, not discovering a new failure class.\n33\t   - The NVE diagnostic is a normalized ratio of critic value to evaluation return — a monitoring metric, not a methodological contribution. Value-to-return ratio monitoring is standard practice; the contribution is the specific threshold calibration (1.5x, AUROC=0.971), which is done in-sample on n=29 runs from two environments. This is a useful empirical observation but falls short of a validated diagnostic tool.\n34\t   - The intervention hierarchy (Table 3) is valuable as structured negative evidence but its conclusion — \"nothing works\" — has limited constructive utility. The paper acknowledges this but still lists it as a primary contribution.\n35\t\n36\t   **Suggestion:** Reframe contributions around two genuinely novel findings: (a) the trained-embedding + multi-rate-exposure causal trigger for imagination-specific value overestimation, and (b) the MPC-vs-imagination architectural boundary. De-emphasize the NVE diagnostic and intervention hierarchy as supporting evidence rather than standalone contributions.\n37\t\n38\t2. **Missing experimental comparison with standard overestimation countermeasures.** Section 5 (Discussion) argues at length why Double Q-learning, TD3-style clipped double Q, and ensemble critics were not implemented, citing engineering barriers (redesigning the lambda-return pipeline, restructuring categorical distribution aggregation). While these barriers are real, a reviewer at a top venue is entitled to ask: *at least try a simplified version*. The paper's central claim is that CIVO is mechanistically distinct from standard overestimation. The strongest evidence for this would be showing that Double Q or ensemble critics fail to prevent CIVO, not arguing that they *would* fail based on mechanistic reasoning. The argument that \"an ensemble of critics would converge to the same wrong value because they share biased imagination data\" is plausible but unverified.\n39\t\n40\t   **Suggestion:** Implement at minimum a dual-critic variant (even without full lambda-return redesign — e.g., take the minimum of two independently trained critics as in TD3). If this fails to prevent CIVO, it would be the strongest possible evidence for the \"measurement bias vs. measurement noise\" distinction claimed in the Discussion. If it succeeds, that is also informative. Either outcome strengthens the paper substantially.\n41\t\n42\t3. **Director (Hafner et al., 2022) is absent from the paper.** Director is the most directly relevant hierarchical Dreamer variant — it performs goal-conditioned hierarchical planning *within DreamerV3's latent space*, which is exactly the architecture this paper studies. The paper cites THICK, Hieros, and LS-Imagine as hierarchical alternatives but omits Director, which is authored by the DreamerV3 creator and represents the canonical approach to temporal abstraction in Dreamer. This is a significant gap. The paper's conclusion that \"hierarchical temporal abstraction may avoid flat-conditioning failure\" should engage with Director's existing demonstration of this principle.\n43\t\n44\t   **Suggestion:** Add Director citation and briefly discuss how it avoids CIVO by using subgoals rather than dt-conditioning within a shared dynamics model.\n45\t\n46\t### Minor\n47\t\n48\t4. **Plan2Explore (Sekar et al., 2020) is not cited.** Plan2Explore is the canonical exploration method within the Dreamer framework and demonstrates how world-model imagination can be leveraged for different purposes. While not directly about temporal abstraction, it is relevant to the broader landscape of imagination-based methods in the Dreamer family and would strengthen the background coverage.\n49\t\n50\t5. **The dual-pathway observation (Pathway A: CIVO vs Pathway B: IRD) is descriptive rather than mechanistic.** The paper observes two qualitatively different collapse modes under dt=1 imagination with trained embedding (008e_v3), distinguishing them by entropy direction. But the characterization is phenomenological: \"entropy increases\" vs \"entropy decreases\" is a symptom, not a mechanism. The paper does not explain *why* two seeds under identical configuration follow different collapse trajectories, beyond invoking capacity loss (Lyle et al., 2022) as a hypothesis. For this to count as a contribution, it needs either a mechanistic explanation or a much larger sample size establishing the statistical properties of the bifurcation.\n51\t\n52\t6. **Statistical power remains a concern.** The paper uses 2-4 seeds per configuration, which the authors acknowledge. For a diagnostic paper where the central phenomenon is seed-dependent (1/4 Walker seeds self-corrects, 2/4 008e_v3 seeds collapse), the stochastic nature of the findings makes it difficult to draw strong conclusions about rates. The AUROC of 0.971 on n=29 is encouraging but the confidence interval (0.904-1.000) reflects the limited sample. The paper cites Agarwal et al. (2021) on statistical methodology but does not fully follow its recommendations (e.g., no IQM, no performance profiles).\n53\t\n54\t7. **TD-MPC and TD-MPC2 coverage is thin.** The paper cites Hansen et al. (2024) for TD-MPC2 primarily through the TAWM lens. A brief discussion of TD-MPC's own value-estimation properties (how it handles model error through its planning mechanism) would strengthen the MPC-vs-imagination argument. The recent TD-M(PC)^2 paper (Lin et al., 2025, which is in the bibliography but not cited in the text) is relevant — it adds policy constraints to TD-MPC, touching on the actor-critic interaction that is central to CIVO.\n55\t\n56\t8. **The paper title changed between the task description and the actual manuscript.** The actual title (\"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in Imagination-Based RL: A Diagnostic Analysis of Variable-Rate DreamerV3\") is an improvement over the earlier version — it is more specific and accurate. However, \"fails\" is a strong universal claim for a paper testing one architectural modification (flat dt-conditioning) on two DMC environments. \"Can fail\" or \"fails under flat conditioning\" would be more precise.\n57\t\n58\t## Questions for Authors\n59\t\n60\t1. Have you attempted even a minimal dual-critic implementation to test whether CIVO persists under Double-Q-style minimum? The mechanistic argument in Section 5 is compelling but unverified experimentally.\n61\t\n62\t2. The Gumbel-Softmax gate experiment (022b, NVE=0.63x) is intriguing — it provides temporal signal availability without trainability and avoids CIVO. Could you elaborate on why this is not a viable solution? The eval (300.7) is lower than baseline but not collapsed. Is this a promising direction or does it have other failure modes?\n63\t\n64\t3. For the MPC-vs-imagination comparison: have you considered running TAWM's dt-conditioning within DreamerV3's architecture but with MPC-style planning (e.g., CEM over the learned model) instead of imagination-based AC? This would isolate the planning algorithm from the architecture more cleanly than the cross-system comparison.\n65\t\n66\t4. The TCN dose-response analysis reveals an \"impossible dose\" dilemma on Cheetah. Does this imply that CIVO severity scales with environment complexity in a way that makes representation-level noise fundamentally inadequate, or could adaptive noise schedules work?\n67\t\n68\t5. Why is Director (Hafner et al., 2022) not discussed? It is the canonical hierarchical extension of DreamerV3 and directly relevant to the paper's recommendation that hierarchical approaches may avoid CIVO.\n69\t\n70\t6. The paper claims CIVO is \"analogous to delusional bias but model-induced rather than policy-class-induced.\" Could you formalize this analogy more precisely? In Lu et al. (2018), delusional bias has a precise definition related to policy-class constraints. What is the analogous formal characterization for CIVO?\n71\t\n72\t## Novelty Assessment\n73\t\n74\t- **CIVO as a failure mode:** Moderately novel. The phenomenon (value overestimation from model error in MBRL) is well-studied; the specific trigger (trained dt-embedding co-adaptation under imagination-based training) is new and non-obvious. The factorial isolation of joint necessity (trained + exposed) is clean. The mechanism is a specific, well-characterized instance of a known failure class rather than a new failure class. **Verdict: incremental but useful novelty.**\n75\t\n76\t- **NVE diagnostic metric:** Low novelty. Value-to-return monitoring is standard; the threshold calibration is in-sample and environment-specific. The AUROC analysis is rigorous given the data but the metric itself is not methodologically new. **Verdict: empirical contribution, not methodological.**\n77\t\n78\t- **Gradient magnitude != causal importance (Section 5.2 / Appendix C):** Moderate novelty in this specific context. The general principle is known from multi-task learning; the specific quantification in DreamerV3's world model (reward head: 38-49%, causally irrelevant; obs decoder: 16-22%, causally critical) is a concrete new data point. **Verdict: useful empirical finding, not a conceptual advance.**\n79\t\n80\t- **Intervention hierarchy:** Low novelty as a framework (testing gradient/output/representation levels is standard ablation methodology). High value as empirical evidence that CIVO is robust to multiple intervention strategies. **Verdict: the negative results are informative but the framework is routine.**\n81\t\n82\t- **MPC vs imagination boundary:** Moderate-to-high novelty. This is the paper's most distinctive contribution — the observation that dt-conditioning succeeds under MPC (TAWM) but fails under imagination-based AC is surprising and actionable. The confound discussion is appropriately scoped. **Verdict: the best contribution in the paper, insufficiently emphasized.**\n83\t\n84\t- **Dual-pathway observation (A: CIVO vs B: IRD):** Low novelty in current form. The observation is descriptive, the sample size is small (n=4 seeds), and no mechanistic explanation is provided beyond hypothesis. **Verdict: interesting observation, not a contribution.**\n85\t\n86\t## Scores\n87\t\n88\t- **Soundness:** 3/4 — The factorial design is rigorous, the causal claims are well-scoped, and the statistics are honestly reported (wide CIs acknowledged). The missing Double-Q experiment is a gap but not a fatal flaw. The TAWM comparison has acknowledged confounds.\n89\t\n90\t- **Contribution:** 2/4 — The CIVO finding is a useful characterization of a specific failure mode, and the MPC-vs-imagination boundary is genuinely informative. However, the overall contribution is narrow: one architectural modification on two environments, with the conclusion that \"it doesn't work and we can't fix it.\" The community benefits are real but limited — practitioners now know to avoid flat dt-conditioning in imagination-based methods, but no constructive solution is offered beyond \"use hierarchy.\"\n91\t\n92\t- **Clarity:** 3/4 — The paper is well-organized with clear findings (F1-F7), good tables, and appropriate scoping of claims. The related work is too compressed (two paragraphs for a diagnostic paper that needs extensive positioning). The appendix is thorough but some material (gradient decomposition, seed-divergence analysis) is important enough for the main text.\n93\t\n94\t- **Overall:** 3/6 (Weak Reject) — The paper presents rigorous negative evidence about a specific design choice (flat dt-conditioning in DreamerV3), identifies a real failure mechanism, and provides useful diagnostics. However, the novelty is incremental relative to existing understanding of value overestimation in MBRL; the experimental scope is narrow (two DMC tasks, one architecture); the most important countermeasures (ensemble critics, Double Q) are discussed but not tried; and the constructive contribution is limited. For a top venue at 25% acceptance, the paper would need either (a) a working mitigation that demonstrates deep understanding, or (b) substantially broader experimental coverage demonstrating that CIVO generalizes beyond DreamerV3/DMC-proprioceptive. The current version falls between rigorous blog post and full paper. A workshop paper or a journal submission with expanded experiments would be more appropriate venues.\n95\t\n96\t- **Confidence:** 4/5 — I am confident in my assessment. I have read the full paper including appendices, verified the related work coverage against the literature, and evaluated the experimental design. My main uncertainty is whether the engineering barriers to implementing Double Q / ensemble critics in DreamerV3 are truly prohibitive; if so, my assessment of Weakness #2 would soften.\n97\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01C87x6XK22ZFfR1vd3R88hn","session_id":"8160c2d3-e1f0-4475-97c0-9800d500973c","uuid":"b021836d-5ab6-483a-99fb-f3068fcb2294","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:52:19.125Z"}
