{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C87x6XK22ZFfR1vd3R88hn","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have comprehensive understanding of the paper. Let me provide the full structured review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: Diagnostic Analysis of Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate DreamerV3\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper investigates why variable-rate imagination (variable dt) fails in DreamerV3, a world-model RL agent that trains actor-critic entirely within imagined rollouts. Through factorial experiments primarily on Walker Walk and Cheetah Run (DMC, proprioceptive observations), the authors identify a failure mode they term Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation (CIVO): when a trained temporal embedding is combined with multi-rate data exposure, the RSSM reward head develops a 2.78x prediction gap between dt=1 and dt=2, which the critic internalizes as optimistic values, ultimately collapsing performance. A Normalized Value-to-Eval ratio (NVE) diagnostic with a 1.5x threshold achieves AUROC=0.971 on n=29 configurations. The paper further evaluates a three-level intervention hierarchy (gradient stopping, output capping, representation noise), finding that no intervention reliably eliminates CIVO. The paper contrasts this failure with TAWM's success under MPC re-planning, attributing the divergence to the feedback architecture (imagination-based bootstrapping vs. re-planning from real states). The paper is positioned as a diagnostic analysis paper targeting CoRL 2026.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Rigorous factorial design with clean causal isolation.** The 2x2 ablation matrix (trained/removed embedding x exposed/unexposed to multi-rate data) is methodologically sound. The frozen-embedding control (015b) and Gumbel-gate control (022b) are particularly well-designed additions that isolate trainability as the necessary condition. The paper follows established factorial methodology precedent (Henderson 2018, Andrychowicz et al. 2021) and clearly separates confounding factors.\n\n2. **The MPC vs. imagination architectural boundary is the most distinctive and actionable contribution.** The observation that dt-conditioning succeeds under TAWM's MPC re-planning but fails under imagination-based actor-critic training is surprising and non-obvious. The mechanistic explanation (MPC re-plans from real states at every step, preventing model errors from compounding into critic bootstrapping targets) is well-articulated. This finding directly informs practitioners about when temporal conditioning is safe to use and when it is not.\n\n3. **The gradient magnitude vs. causal importance dissociation is a concrete, well-quantified finding.** The demonstration that the reward head contributes 38--49% of gradients to the temporal embedding yet is causally irrelevant (reward-only SGS fails with NVE=10.42x), while the observation decoder contributing only 16--22% carries the critical dt-specific spatial information, is a useful empirical data point for the broader multi-head world-model community. The compensatory amplification effect (blocking one pathway shifts co-adaptation to remaining ones) is an important practical insight.\n\n4. **Extensive negative evidence is systematically organized.** The intervention hierarchy table (Table 3) covering 9 configurations across gradient, output, and representation levels, with clear prevention rates (SGS 0/3--1/6, RAVE 0/2, TCN 2/5 on Walker only), provides a comprehensive record of what does not work and why. This saves future researchers from re-exploring these dead ends.\n\n5. **Honest reporting of stochastic outcomes and partial falsification.** The paper transparently reports that collapse is seed-dependent (not all seeds collapse), that the dt=1 imagination mitigation was partially falsified (2/4 seeds collapse via distinct mechanisms), and that even the most successful intervention (TCN sigma=0.2) shows high seed variance (eval 5--91% across seeds). The dual-pathway observation (CIVO Pathway A vs. IRD Pathway B) with distinct entropy signatures adds empirical texture. The paper does not overclaim.\n\n6. **The CIVO mechanism is differentiated from prior overestimation phenomena.** The paper makes a credible case that CIVO differs from standard maximization bias (addressed by Double Q/TD3), from delusional bias (policy-class-induced per Lu et al. 2018), and from MBPO-style model exploitation (mitigated by rollout truncation). The key distinction -- that the RSSM is \"confidently wrong\" at dt>1, producing low-variance biased predictions that uncertainty quantification would not flag -- is well-argued. The random-policy decomposition (36% dynamics bias, 64% actor exploitation) quantitatively substantiates the two-stage mechanism.\n\n7. **Appropriate scoping of claims.** All claims are explicitly scoped to DreamerV3/RSSM and proprioceptive observations. Generalizability to transformer-based world models (DreamerV4), pixel observations, and other architectures is discussed as open questions rather than claimed. This disciplined scoping is appropriate for a diagnostic paper.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n#### Major\n\n1. **(Major) No experimental evaluation of standard overestimation countermeasures.** The Discussion argues that Double Q-learning, ensemble critics, and MBPO-style rollout truncation would not address CIVO because \"an ensemble of critics would converge to the same wrong value because they share biased imagination data.\" This argument is plausible but remains a thought experiment. A minimal dual-critic implementation (even a simplified variant that takes the minimum of two independently trained critics, without full lambda-return pipeline redesign) would either confirm or refute this hypothesis and would be the strongest possible evidence for the paper's central \"measurement bias vs. measurement noise\" distinction. The engineering barriers cited are real but not prohibitive for a diagnostic paper whose central thesis depends on this distinction. This gap weakens the paper's most important mechanistic claim.\n\n2. **(Major) Narrow experimental scope for claims of broad significance.** The primary experiments are conducted on Walker Walk (one locomotion task, proprioceptive observations) with supporting results on Cheetah Run (a second locomotion task) and a single sparse-reward manipulation task (PickCube-v1). Both DMC tasks are relatively simple low-dimensional continuous-control benchmarks. The paper's conclusions about CIVO as a \"structural vulnerability\" of imagination-based RL are based on this limited scope. For a diagnostic paper, breadth is essential to distinguish architecture-specific artifacts from general phenomena. The absence of higher-dimensional tasks, pixel-based observations (which would fundamentally change the gradient decomposition by amplifying the observation decoder channel), and more diverse environment dynamics weakens the generalizability argument. The paper acknowledges this but positions it as future work rather than a limitation of the current contribution.\n\n3. **(Major) The NVE diagnostic is calibrated and validated on the same data.** The 1.5x threshold is derived from n=29 configurations and validated in-sample. While the Youden-optimal threshold (1.53x) independently converging to the preset 1.5x is reassuring, this does not constitute out-of-sample validation. The wide confidence intervals (AUROC 95% CI: 0.904--1.000) reflect the limited statistical power. For the NVE diagnostic to be listed as a contribution (contribution #5 in the introduction), it needs held-out validation on a separate set of environments and configurations. Currently, it is an empirical observation rather than a validated diagnostic tool.\n\n4. **(Major) The intervention hierarchy conclusion (\"nothing works\") has limited constructive value.** The paper's central practical takeaway is that CIVO cannot be mitigated post-hoc and that hierarchical temporal abstraction (THICK, Hieros) is the way forward. But this recommendation is not validated experimentally -- no hierarchical variant is tested. The paper effectively identifies a problem without contributing toward a solution, which is acceptable for a diagnostic paper but limits its impact. The TCN sigma=0.2 result on Walker (2--3 of 4 seeds prevented, but 0% cross-environment transfer) is positioned as a \"diagnostic probe\" rather than a mitigation, which is honest but also reveals that even the authors' best intervention attempt is unreliable.\n\n5. **(Major) Statistical power is insufficient for the paper's seed-dependent findings.** The paper's most interesting findings are stochastic: collapse occurs in 5/6 trained-embedding seeds, self-correction occurs in 1/6, the dt=1 mitigation fails in 2/4 seeds via two distinct mechanisms, and TCN prevents CIVO in 2--3 of 4 Walker seeds but 0 in other environments. With 2--4 seeds per configuration, it is impossible to estimate these rates with meaningful precision. The paper reports these findings transparently but draws conclusions (\"CIVO is universal,\" \"collapse is stochastic,\" \"TCN operates as a binary gate\") that would require substantially larger sample sizes to support. The paper cites Agarwal et al. (2021) but does not follow its recommendations (no IQM, no performance profiles, no stratified bootstrap CIs).\n\n#### Minor\n\n6. **(Minor) The abstract is excessively long and dense.** At approximately 250 words of technical detail packed into a single paragraph, the abstract attempts to summarize all findings including specific numerical results (2.78x reward gap, 27/29 classification, AUROC=0.971, 5/5 sensitivity, 2/9 FPR, 1/6 obs-decoder SGS rate, 2--3 of 4 Walker seeds). This density makes it difficult to extract the paper's core message on first reading. The abstract should lead with the key insight (temporal conditioning succeeds in MPC but fails in imagination-based AC due to CIVO) and defer detailed numbers to the body.\n\n7. **(Minor) Director (Hafner et al., 2022) is absent from the discussion of hierarchical alternatives.** Director is the canonical hierarchical extension within the Dreamer family, performing goal-conditioned hierarchical planning in DreamerV3's latent space. The paper recommends hierarchical approaches as the solution to CIVO and cites THICK, Hieros, and LS-Imagine, but omits the most directly relevant prior work by the DreamerV3 creator. This is a notable gap in the related work.\n\n8. **(Minor) The dual-pathway observation (Pathway A: CIVO vs. Pathway B: IRD) is descriptive rather than mechanistic.** The paper observes two qualitatively different collapse modes under dt=1 imagination with trained embedding, distinguishing them by entropy direction (decreasing vs. increasing). But the characterization is phenomenological -- the paper does not explain why two seeds under identical configuration follow different collapse trajectories, beyond invoking capacity loss (Lyle et al. 2022) as a hypothesis. With only n=4 seeds for this observation, it is premature to present this as a finding rather than a preliminary observation.\n\n9. **(Minor) The paper title overgeneralizes.** \"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in Imagination-Based RL\" implies a general result about all forms of temporal abstraction in all imagination-based methods. The actual finding is specifically about flat dt-conditioning in DreamerV3's RSSM. \"Can fail\" or \"fails under flat conditioning\" would be more precise. Hierarchical temporal abstraction (which the paper itself recommends) is a form of temporal abstraction in imagination-based RL that may not fail.\n\n10. **(Minor) The Discussion section is too compressed.** Key mechanistic arguments (why standard countermeasures fail, the MPC vs. imagination comparison, practitioner guidelines) are condensed into short paragraphs that would benefit from expansion. The gradient decomposition and seed-divergence analyses are important enough for the main text but are relegated to the appendix. For a diagnostic paper, the analysis and discussion are the core contribution and deserve more space.\n\n11. **(Minor) The related work section, while covering the key bases, is only two paragraphs.** For a diagnostic paper that needs extensive positioning against existing overestimation, model exploitation, and temporal abstraction literature, this is insufficient. The distinction from MBPO-style model exploitation and from delusional bias (Lu et al. 2018) deserves more detailed treatment in the related work rather than being deferred to the Discussion.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you attempted even a minimal dual-critic implementation (e.g., training two independent critics and taking the minimum for actor updates) to test whether CIVO persists under Double-Q-style approaches? The mechanistic argument that \"shared biased imagination data\" would defeat ensemble critics is plausible but unverified. Even a pilot experiment on one seed would be informative.\n\n2. The Gumbel-Softmax gate experiment (022b, NVE=0.63x, eval 300.7) provides temporal signal availability without trainability and avoids CIVO. Why is this not discussed as a potential practical solution? The eval is lower than baseline but not collapsed. What are the failure modes of this approach?\n\n3. For the MPC vs. imagination comparison: have you considered implementing CEM-style planning over DreamerV3's learned model (rather than imagination-based AC) with dt-conditioning? This would isolate the planning algorithm from the model architecture more cleanly than the cross-system comparison with TD-MPC2.\n\n4. The TCN dose-response analysis reveals an \"impossible dose\" dilemma on Cheetah where the effective dose exceeds the tolerable dose. Does this imply that CIVO severity scales with environment complexity in a way that makes any representation-level noise intervention fundamentally inadequate? Would adaptive noise schedules (annealing sigma during training) change this picture?\n\n5. Why is Director (Hafner et al., 2022) not discussed as a hierarchical alternative? It is the most directly relevant hierarchical Dreamer variant and represents the canonical approach to temporal abstraction in the DreamerV3 family.\n\n6. The paper claims CIVO is \"analogous to delusional bias but model-induced.\" Can you formalize this analogy more precisely? In Lu et al. (2018), delusional bias has a formal definition. What is the analogous formal characterization for CIVO -- i.e., under what conditions can you guarantee CIVO will occur given specific properties of the world model?\n\n7. The random-policy decomposition (36% dynamics bias, 64% actor exploitation) is one of the strongest mechanistic results. Can you provide confidence intervals for this decomposition and discuss whether the ratio changes across training stages?\n\n---\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n\n- **CIVO as a failure mode:** Moderately novel. The underlying phenomenon (value overestimation from model error in MBRL) is well-studied (MBPO, Lambert et al., Frauenknecht et al.). The novelty lies in identifying the specific trigger (trained dt-embedding co-adaptation + multi-rate exposure) and the specific architectural context (imagination-based AC vs. MPC), not in discovering a new failure class. The factorial isolation of joint necessity is clean. Incremental but useful.\n\n- **MPC vs. imagination architectural boundary:** Moderate-to-high novelty. This is the paper's most distinctive contribution. The observation that dt-conditioning succeeds under MPC but fails under imagination-based AC is surprising, actionable, and not previously documented. Currently under-emphasized in the paper.\n\n- **Gradient magnitude != causal importance:** Low-to-moderate novelty. The general principle is known from multi-task learning literature. The specific quantification in DreamerV3 (reward head 38--49%, obs decoder 16--22%, with compensatory amplification) is a useful empirical data point but not a conceptual advance.\n\n- **NVE diagnostic:** Low novelty. Value-to-return ratio monitoring is standard. The threshold calibration (1.5x, in-sample on n=29) is an empirical observation, not a validated methodology.\n\n- **Intervention hierarchy:** Low novelty as a framework; moderate value as structured negative evidence. The specific failure modes documented (compensatory amplification, EMA anchor pollution, \"impossible dose\") are informative empirical observations.\n\n- **Dual-pathway observation (CIVO vs. IRD):** Low novelty in current form. Descriptive and under-powered (n=4 seeds). No mechanistic explanation beyond hypothesis.\n\n---\n\n### Scores\n\n- **Soundness: 3/4.** The factorial design is rigorous, causal claims are well-scoped, and statistics are honestly reported with appropriate caveats. The missing Double-Q experiment is a real gap. The in-sample NVE validation is a limitation. The seed counts are too low for the stochastic phenomena being characterized.\n\n- **Contribution: 2/4.** The CIVO finding is a useful characterization of a specific failure mode, and the MPC vs. imagination boundary is genuinely informative. However, the overall contribution is narrow: one architectural modification tested on two primary environments with proprioceptive observations, concluding that \"it doesn't work and we can't fix it.\" The community benefit is real (practitioners now know to avoid flat dt-conditioning in imagination-based methods) but limited in scope and constructive impact.\n\n- **Clarity: 3/4.** The paper is well-organized with clear finding labels (F1--F7), informative tables, and appropriate claim scoping. The related work is too compressed. The abstract is too dense. Key analyses (gradient decomposition, seed divergence) are relegated to the appendix despite being central to the argument. The main text Discussion section is too compressed for a diagnostic paper.\n\n- **Significance: 2/4.** The work addresses a real question (why does temporal abstraction fail in world-model RL?) but the answer is narrow (it fails because of a specific co-adaptation between trained dt-embedding and RSSM reward prediction in DreamerV3, under proprioceptive observations). The practical impact is limited to a warning: \"do not use flat dt-conditioning in imagination-based AC.\" The constructive recommendation (use hierarchical approaches) is not validated. The finding is unlikely to influence the broader world-model RL research direction, which is already moving toward hierarchical architectures and transformer-based models.\n\n- **Overall: 4/10 (Borderline Reject).** The paper presents rigorous negative evidence about a specific design choice in DreamerV3, identifies a real failure mechanism with clean causal isolation, and provides useful diagnostics. The MPC vs. imagination boundary is a genuinely informative contribution. However, the novelty is incremental relative to existing understanding of value overestimation in MBRL; the experimental scope is narrow (two DMC locomotion tasks, proprioceptive observations, one base architecture); the most important countermeasures (ensemble critics, Double Q) are discussed but not tried; and the constructive contribution is limited to \"use hierarchy\" without validation. The paper falls between a rigorous technical report and a full conference contribution. Strengthening the paper would require either (a) implementing and testing standard overestimation countermeasures to verify the \"measurement bias\" claim, (b) substantially broader experimental coverage (more environments, pixel observations, potentially a second base architecture), or (c) a validated mitigation demonstrating the diagnostic understanding translates to a working solution.\n\n- **Confidence: 4/5.** I have read the complete paper including all appendix sections, verified the related work coverage, and evaluated the experimental design against the claims. My main uncertainty concerns whether the engineering barriers to implementing Double Q / ensemble critics in DreamerV3's categorical critic architecture are truly prohibitive -- if so, the assessment of Weakness #1 would soften somewhat."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a47e2900231f7f94c (use SendMessage with to: 'a47e2900231f7f94c' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 104759\ntool_uses: 26\nduration_ms: 183483</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8160c2d3-e1f0-4475-97c0-9800d500973c","uuid":"a52c1d2a-a28b-4632-a822-7aad012ae8ff","timestamp":"2026-05-20T21:54:21.016Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Please provide your complete detailed review in full structured format. I need the complete text including all sections: Summary, Strengths (numbered), Weaknesses (numbered with Major/Minor labels), Questions for Authors (numbered), Novelty Assessment (itemized by contribution), and Scores with justifications. Do not abbreviate or summarize — give the full review text.","agentId":"a47e2900231f7f94c","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have comprehensive understanding of the paper. Let me provide the full structured review.\n\n---\n\n## Review: Diagnostic Analysis of Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate DreamerV3\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper investigates why variable-rate imagination (variable dt) fails in DreamerV3, a world-model RL agent that trains actor-critic entirely within imagined rollouts. Through factorial experiments primarily on Walker Walk and Cheetah Run (DMC, proprioceptive observations), the authors identify a failure mode they term Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation (CIVO): when a trained temporal embedding is combined with multi-rate data exposure, the RSSM reward head develops a 2.78x prediction gap between dt=1 and dt=2, which the critic internalizes as optimistic values, ultimately collapsing performance. A Normalized Value-to-Eval ratio (NVE) diagnostic with a 1.5x threshold achieves AUROC=0.971 on n=29 configurations. The paper further evaluates a three-level intervention hierarchy (gradient stopping, output capping, representation noise), finding that no intervention reliably eliminates CIVO. The paper contrasts this failure with TAWM's success under MPC re-planning, attributing the divergence to the feedback architecture (imagination-based bootstrapping vs. re-planning from real states). The paper is positioned as a diagnostic analysis paper targeting CoRL 2026.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Rigorous factorial design with clean causal isolation.** The 2x2 ablation matrix (trained/removed embedding x exposed/unexposed to multi-rate data) is methodologically sound. The frozen-embedding control (015b) and Gumbel-gate control (022b) are particularly well-designed additions that isolate trainability as the necessary condition. The paper follows established factorial methodology precedent (Henderson 2018, Andrychowicz et al. 2021) and clearly separates confounding factors.\n\n2. **The MPC vs. imagination architectural boundary is the most distinctive and actionable contribution.** The observation that dt-conditioning succeeds under TAWM's MPC re-planning but fails under imagination-based actor-critic training is surprising and non-obvious. The mechanistic explanation (MPC re-plans from real states at every step, preventing model errors from compounding into critic bootstrapping targets) is well-articulated. This finding directly informs practitioners about when temporal conditioning is safe to use and when it is not.\n\n3. **The gradient magnitude vs. causal importance dissociation is a concrete, well-quantified finding.** The demonstration that the reward head contributes 38--49% of gradients to the temporal embedding yet is causally irrelevant (reward-only SGS fails with NVE=10.42x), while the observation decoder contributing only 16--22% carries the critical dt-specific spatial information, is a useful empirical data point for the broader multi-head world-model community. The compensatory amplification effect (blocking one pathway shifts co-adaptation to remaining ones) is an important practical insight.\n\n4. **Extensive negative evidence is systematically organized.** The intervention hierarchy table (Table 3) covering 9 configurations across gradient, output, and representation levels, with clear prevention rates (SGS 0/3--1/6, RAVE 0/2, TCN 2/5 on Walker only), provides a comprehensive record of what does not work and why. This saves future researchers from re-exploring these dead ends.\n\n5. **Honest reporting of stochastic outcomes and partial falsification.** The paper transparently reports that collapse is seed-dependent (not all seeds collapse), that the dt=1 imagination mitigation was partially falsified (2/4 seeds collapse via distinct mechanisms), and that even the most successful intervention (TCN sigma=0.2) shows high seed variance (eval 5--91% across seeds). The dual-pathway observation (CIVO Pathway A vs. IRD Pathway B) with distinct entropy signatures adds empirical texture. The paper does not overclaim.\n\n6. **The CIVO mechanism is differentiated from prior overestimation phenomena.** The paper makes a credible case that CIVO differs from standard maximization bias (addressed by Double Q/TD3), from delusional bias (policy-class-induced per Lu et al. 2018), and from MBPO-style model exploitation (mitigated by rollout truncation). The key distinction -- that the RSSM is \"confidently wrong\" at dt>1, producing low-variance biased predictions that uncertainty quantification would not flag -- is well-argued. The random-policy decomposition (36% dynamics bias, 64% actor exploitation) quantitatively substantiates the two-stage mechanism.\n\n7. **Appropriate scoping of claims.** All claims are explicitly scoped to DreamerV3/RSSM and proprioceptive observations. Generalizability to transformer-based world models (DreamerV4), pixel observations, and other architectures is discussed as open questions rather than claimed. This disciplined scoping is appropriate for a diagnostic paper.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n#### Major\n\n1. **(Major) No experimental evaluation of standard overestimation countermeasures.** The Discussion argues that Double Q-learning, ensemble critics, and MBPO-style rollout truncation would not address CIVO because \"an ensemble of critics would converge to the same wrong value because they share biased imagination data.\" This argument is plausible but remains a thought experiment. A minimal dual-critic implementation (even a simplified variant that takes the minimum of two independently trained critics, without full lambda-return pipeline redesign) would either confirm or refute this hypothesis and would be the strongest possible evidence for the paper's central \"measurement bias vs. measurement noise\" distinction. The engineering barriers cited are real but not prohibitive for a diagnostic paper whose central thesis depends on this distinction. This gap weakens the paper's most important mechanistic claim.\n\n2. **(Major) Narrow experimental scope for claims of broad significance.** The primary experiments are conducted on Walker Walk (one locomotion task, proprioceptive observations) with supporting results on Cheetah Run (a second locomotion task) and a single sparse-reward manipulation task (PickCube-v1). Both DMC tasks are relatively simple low-dimensional continuous-control benchmarks. The paper's conclusions about CIVO as a \"structural vulnerability\" of imagination-based RL are based on this limited scope. For a diagnostic paper, breadth is essential to distinguish architecture-specific artifacts from general phenomena. The absence of higher-dimensional tasks, pixel-based observations (which would fundamentally change the gradient decomposition by amplifying the observation decoder channel), and more diverse environment dynamics weakens the generalizability argument. The paper acknowledges this but positions it as future work rather than a limitation of the current contribution.\n\n3. **(Major) The NVE diagnostic is calibrated and validated on the same data.** The 1.5x threshold is derived from n=29 configurations and validated in-sample. While the Youden-optimal threshold (1.53x) independently converging to the preset 1.5x is reassuring, this does not constitute out-of-sample validation. The wide confidence intervals (AUROC 95% CI: 0.904--1.000) reflect the limited statistical power. For the NVE diagnostic to be listed as a contribution (contribution #5 in the introduction), it needs held-out validation on a separate set of environments and configurations. Currently, it is an empirical observation rather than a validated diagnostic tool.\n\n4. **(Major) The intervention hierarchy conclusion (\"nothing works\") has limited constructive value.** The paper's central practical takeaway is that CIVO cannot be mitigated post-hoc and that hierarchical temporal abstraction (THICK, Hieros) is the way forward. But this recommendation is not validated experimentally -- no hierarchical variant is tested. The paper effectively identifies a problem without contributing toward a solution, which is acceptable for a diagnostic paper but limits its impact. The TCN sigma=0.2 result on Walker (2--3 of 4 seeds prevented, but 0% cross-environment transfer) is positioned as a \"diagnostic probe\" rather than a mitigation, which is honest but also reveals that even the authors' best intervention attempt is unreliable.\n\n5. **(Major) Statistical power is insufficient for the paper's seed-dependent findings.** The paper's most interesting findings are stochastic: collapse occurs in 5/6 trained-embedding seeds, self-correction occurs in 1/6, the dt=1 mitigation fails in 2/4 seeds via two distinct mechanisms, and TCN prevents CIVO in 2--3 of 4 Walker seeds but 0 in other environments. With 2--4 seeds per configuration, it is impossible to estimate these rates with meaningful precision. The paper reports these findings transparently but draws conclusions (\"CIVO is universal,\" \"collapse is stochastic,\" \"TCN operates as a binary gate\") that would require substantially larger sample sizes to support. The paper cites Agarwal et al. (2021) but does not follow its recommendations (no IQM, no performance profiles, no stratified bootstrap CIs).\n\n#### Minor\n\n6. **(Minor) The abstract is excessively long and dense.** At approximately 250 words of technical detail packed into a single paragraph, the abstract attempts to summarize all findings including specific numerical results (2.78x reward gap, 27/29 classification, AUROC=0.971, 5/5 sensitivity, 2/9 FPR, 1/6 obs-decoder SGS rate, 2--3 of 4 Walker seeds). This density makes it difficult to extract the paper's core message on first reading. The abstract should lead with the key insight (temporal conditioning succeeds in MPC but fails in imagination-based AC due to CIVO) and defer detailed numbers to the body.\n\n7. **(Minor) Director (Hafner et al., 2022) is absent from the discussion of hierarchical alternatives.** Director is the canonical hierarchical extension within the Dreamer family, performing goal-conditioned hierarchical planning in DreamerV3's latent space. The paper recommends hierarchical approaches as the solution to CIVO and cites THICK, Hieros, and LS-Imagine, but omits the most directly relevant prior work by the DreamerV3 creator. This is a notable gap in the related work.\n\n8. **(Minor) The dual-pathway observation (Pathway A: CIVO vs. Pathway B: IRD) is descriptive rather than mechanistic.** The paper observes two qualitatively different collapse modes under dt=1 imagination with trained embedding, distinguishing them by entropy direction (decreasing vs. increasing). But the characterization is phenomenological -- the paper does not explain why two seeds under identical configuration follow different collapse trajectories, beyond invoking capacity loss (Lyle et al. 2022) as a hypothesis. With only n=4 seeds for this observation, it is premature to present this as a finding rather than a preliminary observation.\n\n9. **(Minor) The paper title overgeneralizes.** \"Why Temporal Abstraction Fails in Imagination-Based RL\" implies a general result about all forms of temporal abstraction in all imagination-based methods. The actual finding is specifically about flat dt-conditioning in DreamerV3's RSSM. \"Can fail\" or \"fails under flat conditioning\" would be more precise. Hierarchical temporal abstraction (which the paper itself recommends) is a form of temporal abstraction in imagination-based RL that may not fail.\n\n10. **(Minor) The Discussion section is too compressed.** Key mechanistic arguments (why standard countermeasures fail, the MPC vs. imagination comparison, practitioner guidelines) are condensed into short paragraphs that would benefit from expansion. The gradient decomposition and seed-divergence analyses are important enough for the main text but are relegated to the appendix. For a diagnostic paper, the analysis and discussion are the core contribution and deserve more space.\n\n11. **(Minor) The related work section, while covering the key bases, is only two paragraphs.** For a diagnostic paper that needs extensive positioning against existing overestimation, model exploitation, and temporal abstraction literature, this is insufficient. The distinction from MBPO-style model exploitation and from delusional bias (Lu et al. 2018) deserves more detailed treatment in the related work rather than being deferred to the Discussion.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you attempted even a minimal dual-critic implementation (e.g., training two independent critics and taking the minimum for actor updates) to test whether CIVO persists under Double-Q-style approaches? The mechanistic argument that \"shared biased imagination data\" would defeat ensemble critics is plausible but unverified. Even a pilot experiment on one seed would be informative.\n\n2. The Gumbel-Softmax gate experiment (022b, NVE=0.63x, eval 300.7) provides temporal signal availability without trainability and avoids CIVO. Why is this not discussed as a potential practical solution? The eval is lower than baseline but not collapsed. What are the failure modes of this approach?\n\n3. For the MPC vs. imagination comparison: have you considered implementing CEM-style planning over DreamerV3's learned model (rather than imagination-based AC) with dt-conditioning? This would isolate the planning algorithm from the model architecture more cleanly than the cross-system comparison with TD-MPC2.\n\n4. The TCN dose-response analysis reveals an \"impossible dose\" dilemma on Cheetah where the effective dose exceeds the tolerable dose. Does this imply that CIVO severity scales with environment complexity in a way that makes any representation-level noise intervention fundamentally inadequate? Would adaptive noise schedules (annealing sigma during training) change this picture?\n\n5. Why is Director (Hafner et al., 2022) not discussed as a hierarchical alternative? It is the most directly relevant hierarchical Dreamer variant and represents the canonical approach to temporal abstraction in the DreamerV3 family.\n\n6. The paper claims CIVO is \"analogous to delusional bias but model-induced.\" Can you formalize this analogy more precisely? In Lu et al. (2018), delusional bias has a formal definition. What is the analogous formal characterization for CIVO -- i.e., under what conditions can you guarantee CIVO will occur given specific properties of the world model?\n\n7. The random-policy decomposition (36% dynamics bias, 64% actor exploitation) is one of the strongest mechanistic results. Can you provide confidence intervals for this decomposition and discuss whether the ratio changes across training stages?\n\n---\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n\n- **CIVO as a failure mode:** Moderately novel. The underlying phenomenon (value overestimation from model error in MBRL) is well-studied (MBPO, Lambert et al., Frauenknecht et al.). The novelty lies in identifying the specific trigger (trained dt-embedding co-adaptation + multi-rate exposure) and the specific architectural context (imagination-based AC vs. MPC), not in discovering a new failure class. The factorial isolation of joint necessity is clean. Incremental but useful.\n\n- **MPC vs. imagination architectural boundary:** Moderate-to-high novelty. This is the paper's most distinctive contribution. The observation that dt-conditioning succeeds under MPC but fails under imagination-based AC is surprising, actionable, and not previously documented. Currently under-emphasized in the paper.\n\n- **Gradient magnitude != causal importance:** Low-to-moderate novelty. The general principle is known from multi-task learning literature. The specific quantification in DreamerV3 (reward head 38--49%, obs decoder 16--22%, with compensatory amplification) is a useful empirical data point but not a conceptual advance.\n\n- **NVE diagnostic:** Low novelty. Value-to-return ratio monitoring is standard. The threshold calibration (1.5x, in-sample on n=29) is an empirical observation, not a validated methodology.\n\n- **Intervention hierarchy:** Low novelty as a framework; moderate value as structured negative evidence. The specific failure modes documented (compensatory amplification, EMA anchor pollution, \"impossible dose\") are informative empirical observations.\n\n- **Dual-pathway observation (CIVO vs. IRD):** Low novelty in current form. Descriptive and under-powered (n=4 seeds). No mechanistic explanation beyond hypothesis.\n\n---\n\n### Scores\n\n- **Soundness: 3/4.** The factorial design is rigorous, causal claims are well-scoped, and statistics are honestly reported with appropriate caveats. The missing Double-Q experiment is a real gap. The in-sample NVE validation is a limitation. The seed counts are too low for the stochastic phenomena being characterized.\n\n- **Contribution: 2/4.** The CIVO finding is a useful characterization of a specific failure mode, and the MPC vs. imagination boundary is genuinely informative. However, the overall contribution is narrow: one architectural modification tested on two primary environments with proprioceptive observations, concluding that \"it doesn't work and we can't fix it.\" The community benefit is real (practitioners now know to avoid flat dt-conditioning in imagination-based methods) but limited in scope and constructive impact.\n\n- **Clarity: 3/4.** The paper is well-organized with clear finding labels (F1--F7), informative tables, and appropriate claim scoping. The related work is too compressed. The abstract is too dense. Key analyses (gradient decomposition, seed divergence) are relegated to the appendix despite being central to the argument. The main text Discussion section is too compressed for a diagnostic paper.\n\n- **Significance: 2/4.** The work addresses a real question (why does temporal abstraction fail in world-model RL?) but the answer is narrow (it fails because of a specific co-adaptation between trained dt-embedding and RSSM reward prediction in DreamerV3, under proprioceptive observations). The practical impact is limited to a warning: \"do not use flat dt-conditioning in imagination-based AC.\" The constructive recommendation (use hierarchical approaches) is not validated. The finding is unlikely to influence the broader world-model RL research direction, which is already moving toward hierarchical architectures and transformer-based models.\n\n- **Overall: 4/10 (Borderline Reject).** The paper presents rigorous negative evidence about a specific design choice in DreamerV3, identifies a real failure mechanism with clean causal isolation, and provides useful diagnostics. The MPC vs. imagination boundary is a genuinely informative contribution. However, the novelty is incremental relative to existing understanding of value overestimation in MBRL; the experimental scope is narrow (two DMC locomotion tasks, proprioceptive observations, one base architecture); the most important countermeasures (ensemble critics, Double Q) are discussed but not tried; and the constructive contribution is limited to \"use hierarchy\" without validation. The paper falls between a rigorous technical report and a full conference contribution. Strengthening the paper would require either (a) implementing and testing standard overestimation countermeasures to verify the \"measurement bias\" claim, (b) substantially broader experimental coverage (more environments, pixel observations, potentially a second base architecture), or (c) a validated mitigation demonstrating the diagnostic understanding translates to a working solution.\n\n- **Confidence: 4/5.** I have read the complete paper including all appendix sections, verified the related work coverage, and evaluated the experimental design against the claims. My main uncertainty concerns whether the engineering barriers to implementing Double Q / ensemble critics in DreamerV3's categorical critic architecture are truly prohibitive -- if so, the assessment of Weakness #1 would soften somewhat."}],"totalDurationMs":183483,"totalTokens":104759,"totalToolUseCount":26,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4226,"cache_read_input_tokens":96035,"output_tokens":4497,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4226},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":4497,"cache_read_input_tokens":96035,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4226,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4226,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":11,"searchCount":6,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":9}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GhrZ86qCkjV6qLipLfKHVa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have both complete reviews. Let me organize them:\n\n**R1 (Methods + Experiments)**: 4/6 (Borderline Accept)\n- Soundness: 2/4, Contribution: 3/4, Clarity: 3/4\n- Key concerns: low seed counts, in-sample NVE validation, missing standard overestimation baselines (Double Q/ensemble), treatment duration asymmetry\n\n**R2 (Novelty + Related Work)**: 3/6 (Weak Reject) - wait, the R2 full review says \"Overall: 4/10 (Borderline Reject)\" but earlier the summary said 3/6. \n\nI need to reconcile the scale discrepancy. The R2 agent used a 10-point scale in the full review but 6-point scale in the summary, so I'll stick with 3/6 (Weak Reject) for consistency. Now I'm moving to cross-sharing where each reviewer reads the other's assessment and responds, then I'll identify where they converge and diverge before synthesizing a final recommendation.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4973,"cache_read_input_tokens":68563,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4973},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8160c2d3-e1f0-4475-97c0-9800d500973c","uuid":"5f7d3865-634f-46c9-b089-d60c5ebca307"}
